Thank you for reporting this issue\! I've identified the root cause of the error.

## 🔍 Root Cause Analysis

The error occurs in the `CollectiveMemory` class's garbage collection method. The issue is that `this.cache` is initialized as an `OptimizedLRUCache` instance (line 183), but the code at line 749 attempts to call `forEach()` directly on it:

```javascript
// Line 749 - Current problematic code
this.cache.forEach((value, key) => {
  if (now - value.timestamp > cacheTimeout) {
    this.cache.delete(key);
  }
});
```

The `OptimizedLRUCache` class doesn't have a `forEach` method - it internally uses a Map stored in `this.cache.cache`.

## 🛠️ Solution

The fix is straightforward - we need to access the internal Map:

```javascript
// Line 749 - Fixed code
this.cache.cache.forEach((value, key) => {
  if (now - value.timestamp > cacheTimeout) {
    this.cache.cache.delete(key);
  }
});
```

However, a cleaner solution would be to add a `forEach` method to the `OptimizedLRUCache` class:

```javascript
// Add to OptimizedLRUCache class
forEach(callback) {
  this.cache.forEach(callback);
}

delete(key) {
  if (this.cache.has(key)) {
    const entry = this.cache.get(key);
    this.cache.delete(key);
    this.currentMemory -= entry.size;
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## 📋 Next Steps

I'll create a PR with the fix shortly. This will resolve the TypeError and ensure the garbage collection process works correctly in the hive-mind memory system.

## 🔧 Temporary Workaround

If you need an immediate fix while waiting for the PR, you can manually edit line 749 in `/src/cli/simple-commands/hive-mind/memory.js` to use `this.cache.cache.forEach` instead of `this.cache.forEach`.

